# Guide: add a new page

Walks through adding a static "About" page — a second full page alongside `index.html`, not an HTMX partial. Follow this shape for any new page; for a new *interactive element on an existing page*, see the `htmx` skill's `guides/add-an-htmx-action.md` instead.

## 1. Add the template

Create `src/{{ package_name }}/templates/about.html`:

```html
{% raw %}{% extends "base.html" %}

{% block title %}About{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<div class="card">
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p>...</p>
</div>
{% endblock %}{% endraw +%}
```

{% raw %}{% extends %}{% endraw %} has to be the first line — nothing, not even a blank line or comment, can precede it.

## 2. Add the route

{% if framework == "fastapi" %}
In `src/{{ package_name }}/routes/todos.py` (or a new `routes/pages.py` if this is growing past one file):

```python
@router.get("/about", response_class=HTMLResponse)
def about(request: Request) -> HTMLResponse:
    return templates.TemplateResponse(request, "about.html", {})
```
{% else %}
In `src/{{ package_name }}/routes/todos.py` (or a new `routes/pages.py` if this is growing past one file):

```python
@bp.get("/about")
def about():
    return render_template("about.html")
```
{% endif %}

If you split routes into a new module, register it in `main.py` the same way `todos`{% if framework == "fastapi" %}'s router{% else %}'s blueprint{% endif %} already is.

## 3. Link to it

Add an `<a href="/about">About</a>` wherever makes sense — a plain link, no HTMX attributes needed, since this is a full page navigation, not a partial swap. If you want it to feel like an in-page transition instead of a full reload, that's what `hx-boost` is for; see the `htmx` skill's `references/attributes-and-swaps.md`.
